Phosphor Guava
Ingredients: TRAP & DRILL + VIBRANT, TROPICAL & SUNNY + Alto Saxophone  
Creative Catalyst: In rat-infested cinema booths across sun-bleached archipelago ports, eccentric projectionists scored non-existent silent celluloid epics in real time using skittering drum machines and blistering brass.

Phosphor Guava sounds like blinding midday sunlight refracted through boiling 35mm film stock. Subterranean 808 glide basses warp and weave beneath frantic, syncopated Chicago-and-UK drill hi-hats, abruptly colliding with jubilant Caribbean steel pans and brassy, virtuosic alto saxophone riffs that screech, flutter, and soar with euphoric carnival intensity.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 140–144 BPM (operating in a swaggering 70–72 BPM half-time feel). The pulse counterpoints relentless triplet drill skitters with an infectious, hip-rolling Antillean soca bounce that refuses to stay dark.
- Harmonic Palette: Lydian dominant and Mixolydian modes awash in dazzling major triads, chromatic horn squeals, and microtonally pitch-bent island brass that mimics the unstable speed of hand-cranked projectors.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Shutter-Chop Gating: Alto saxophone runs abruptly sliced at 24-frames-per-second rhythmic intervals via optical tremolo units, imitating flickering projector gates.
  2. Celluloid Glide 808s: Deep, sliding sub-bass driven through scorching projector vacuum tubes until the low-end glows with fuzzy, saturated warmth.
  3. Cistern-Splash Reverb: Shimmering convolution reverbs captured inside open-air zinc water towers and marble art-deco movie foyers, drenching woodwinds in humid, liquid daylight.
- Dynamics: Abrupt jump-cuts between humid, ticking silence (the whir of an empty film spool) and maximalist, wall-shaking daylight explosions of horns and distorted slides.

Scene & Origin Lore
The sound caught fire in the dilapidated cinema houses of Port-de-Paix and San Juan during long tropical blackouts in the late 1990s. The movement's pioneers—calling themselves Les Bobiniers (The Spoolers)—refused to close down when imports of silent film reels dried up; instead, they looped clear, scratched leader-tape over blazing carbon-arc lamps and improvised raucous soundtracks to imaginary storylines projected straight into the open sea breeze. Scene dress is functional and theatrical: bleached khaki safari coats slung over bare chests, bandoliers woven from melted 35mm film stock, gold teeth, and industrial welding spectacles fitted with mango-tinted glass. Performance rituals demand that before the first 808 drop, the saxophonist pours a shot of overproof sugarcane rum directly down the horn's bell into the projector motor to lubricate the gears and bless the imaginary picture.

Typical Instruments: Vintage Selmer Mark VI Alto Saxophone (amplified through analog tape-echo and wah pedals), Roland TR-808, modified Bell & Howell 16mm projector optical sensors (converting light flickers into rhythmic clicks), pan-Caribbean steel pans, marimbula, hollowed guava-wood log drums, and overdriven Akai S950 samplers.

